<body>
  <main class="lesson-shell" aria-label="Lesson 8: Tongue Position">
    <article class="lesson-card">
      <div class="lesson-inner">
        <header class="lesson-header">
          <p class="eyebrow">Facial Volume Harmony</p>
          <h1>Lesson 8: Tongue Position</h1>
          <div class="intro-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </header>

        <section class="lesson-content">
          <p class="lead">One of the key elements of the practice is developing awareness of the tongue’s position within the oral cavity. In everyday life, the tongue is recommended to rest in an elevated position, in contact with the palate.</p>

          <p>It is important to softly and gradually place the tongue along the palate from the tip toward the back, as widely as feels comfortable and natural for your individual anatomy.</p>

          
          <figure class="image-wrap">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Tongue posture illustration showing incorrect and correct tongue positions." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </figure>

          <p>The tip of the tongue should not press against the teeth. Instead, it should be placed behind the front teeth, in the area where the palate begins to rise (see image). This position helps create a sense of support and functional contact with the upper part of the oral cavity.</p>

          
          <figure class="image-wrap">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Palate image showing tongue placement area." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </figure>

          <p>The middle and back portions of the tongue gently spread along the palate, without excessive tension. The contact should feel stable, yet comfortable.</p>

          <p>It is important to understand that individual anatomy varies from person to person. The exact distance from the teeth or how completely the tongue covers the palate is not critical, especially if this awareness is new to you.</p>

          <p>The main goal of the practice is to develop a more conscious and functional contact between the tongue and the palate, always within the limits of your own anatomy, supporting a sense of balance and stability in the facial area.</p>

          <p>At no point should the tip of the tongue press against or push on the teeth. All sensations should remain soft and comfortable.</p>

          <section class="section">
            <h2>Swallowing:</h2>
            <p>When swallowing saliva (outside of eating), it is recommended to swallow without lifting the tongue away from the palate.</p>
            <p>You may intentionally practice this for a few minutes by taking a small sip of water and swallowing while maintaining gentle tongue contact with the palate.</p>
          </section>

          <section class="section">
            <h2>Assignment</h2>
            <ul>
              <li>Throughout the day, regularly check whether your tongue is resting in its proper position.</li>
              <li>Pay attention to nasal breathing.</li>
              <li>Bring awareness to swallowing.</li>
              <li>In addition, continue practicing the relaxation exercises for the facial muscles and the head.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>
        </section>
      </div>
